 The five inputs isolate what the classifier receives. Raw coordinates $R$ preserve the original position. Direction-only inputs $D_0$, $D_M$ and $D_A$ discard radius about three origins: $(0,0)$, the midpoint $(0.5,h/2)$ and the upper origin $(0,3)$. The control $P_A$ retains both direction and radius about $(0,3)$, which together reconstruct the original coordinates. Directions use exact division by the nonzero norm, with zero residual mapped to zero. All inputs are then standardized from training-set means and population standard deviations; constant features use scale one. This positive affine transformation retains the information in each representation.
 
-All models shared a 64-unit tanh hidden layer, linear output and training schedule, yet two-input models had 257 trainable parameters versus 321 for three-input models. The Bayes-floor comparison isolates information loss; fitted-model comparisons retain this capacity difference. We fitted class labels $-1/+1$ using mean squared error (MSE), assigning positive outputs to the second class. Full-batch Adam~\citep{Kingma2015} used $(\beta_1,\beta_2)=(0.9,0.999)$, optimizer $\epsilon=10^{-8}$, and learning rates $0.001$ and $0.01$. Every candidate executed 4,000 updates.\footnote{The stopping criterion was completion of the fixed update budget, including candidates whose selected checkpoint occurred earlier.} Validation was evaluated at initialization and every 50 updates. Selection minimized validation error, followed by validation MSE, earlier update and lower learning rate.
+All models shared a 64-unit tanh hidden layer, linear output and training schedule, yet two-input models had 257 trainable parameters versus 321 for three-input models. The Bayes-floor comparison isolates information loss; fitted-model comparisons retain this capacity difference. We fitted class labels $-1/+1$ using mean squared error (MSE), assigning positive outputs to the second class. Full-batch Adam~\citep{Kingma2015} used $(\beta_1,\beta_2)=(0.9,0.999)$, optimizer $\epsilon=10^{-8}$, and learning rates $0.001$ and $0.01$. Every candidate executed\vadjust{\nobreak} 4,000 updates.\footnote{The stopping criterion was completion of the fixed update budget, including candidates whose selected checkpoint occurred earlier.} Validation was evaluated at initialization and every 50 updates. Selection minimized validation error, followed by validation MSE, earlier update and lower learning rate.
 
 The main design required 300 candidate fits. At $h=1$, width-15 and unconditioned width-64 diagnostics each added 60 fits for $R,D_A,P_A$. These 420 final candidate fits were separate from a 48-fit development stage using seeds 101 and 103. Training and analysis protocols were frozen before final test generation; the 210 selected models were then evaluated once. 
 
